t a time that we we we see  more interesting biographies written of  women political figures um and  particularly from from asia than than  than we've seen there was a really  interesting biography last year of um  um  written by the woman who wrote uh  yeah and uh that's you know it seems  like there's there's a different  generation of women who have come of age  after after the victories of feminism um  offering very interesting perspectives  that would not have been available i  think uh you know 30 and 40 years ago so  that's that's wonderful and i i think  it's a terrific book um just very happy  to  have been able to uh to read it and  let's see does anyone have any questions  in the audience  i think there's a we have a mic if  um  yes hi  um you've talked um a little bit about  how madame news  recollections might have been colored by  the trauma that she experienced and i  wondered how you dealt with  the unreliability of this source um  while you were working on this project  that's a great question so um madame new  was an unreliable narrator and i  in writing my book i chose  sort of the unorthodox path of putting  myself in the book because i wanted to  be  someone who could see both sides i mean  i did the research in the archives in  france i did the research into the  materials here in the united states and  i think there are no real objective  views of madame new that what was  written about her in the 60s was also  slanted by the  mostly white male reporters who were  writing about her  and so  from both of these sides i had to kind  of navigate okay  what is what is true what is false and  and in her memoirs that she wrote where  she's the center of the universe  obviously that wasn't going to be much  help to me but what was was finding out  what kind of shoes she was wearing sort  of the little details that were  factual that i wasn't going to get any  place else  but you're right that was a really  tricky thing and i tried to be as honest  as i can in the book about  walking that fine line between believing  what she says and also making sure it's  factually correct  the photographs i mean i mentioned this  earlier really really quite incredible  and the and um and i noticed that a  couple of them came from her own  collection is that  did she give you those photographs so  those were part of the memoir to be um  published for clinton okay yeah okay  uh sir do you have a question yes thank  you first off eric and monique thanks a  lot for a very great program  uh my question though i'm i'm curious  about what you think how the press did  the u.s press because you mentioned the  vietnamese press how it was under the  thumb of the vietnamese government how  much  better did the american press do because  you must have read a lot of articles and  watched a lot of news clips you know the  the us media did they build her up or  demonize her or trivialize her or you  know how did the u.s press do that's  such a good question um  the u.s press when you know i think if  you read the accounts of albert stam and  and sheehan and malcolm brown who were  there  in those early days um they they tend to  have really believed that the united  states was doing the right thing by  being in vietnam this was a country that  needed to be saved that the dominoes  were very real they were really falling  and um and so they were really  sort of patriotically behind the united  states involvement in south vietnam um  but because of that madame new and her  family were really a stumbling block i  mean they were screwing things up right  and left and these reporters could see  it and no one else was talking about it  and so they were  advocates for  i don't want to say they were advocates  for regime change but they were  advocates for  getting america more involved in south  vietnam reporting the facts as they saw  them which  was hard to do in that in that context  i don't think that they i don't think  they built madame new up i i really  don't think that they liked her very  much  and  should point out too that she read them  religiously and there's there's a point  in the in in the book where you tell her  that david halverstem has just been  killed in a car accident he was he was  in a car accident in 2008 or something  like that and you you tell her that you  break that news to her and she's  actually seems kind of kind of saddened  by it yeah so as if she had been it was  a personal friend of her someone she'd  known well  he he said something about her like she  was the only one in the family who knew  how to do a parade you know she raised  her hand like mussolini you know she he  said these things that in any other  reading would be um  not compliments but she was sort of like  oh i remember him he was a good reporter  and he always told the truth  on that note i think we're about out of  time i want to thank you very much and  please uh  outside the books are for sale we hope  you'll pick up a copy thank you  all right thank you everybody for  attending thanks again to uh mr banks  and miss demery copies of finding the  dragon lady are on sale now out in the  main auditorium and miss demery will be  signing books just outside of the  auditorium thanks everyone enjoy the lit  fest  yes  [Music]  [Music]  yeah well i drink mine  that was monique brinson demery talking  about the search for vietnam's madame  new we'll be back with more shortly  oh  [Music]
 

